TYPES.md §5.1 (index `xs[i]`: the key type for a keyed list; a key argument of hasKey, get and find), §5.3, §7.4: a literal key is checked against the list's key type, refinement included, so an integer literal outside a refined key's bounds is E3204 at check time, never only E4002 at evaluation: `rs[20]` and `rs.find(20)` (whose binding drops the refinement; the key type keeps it) on a list keyed by an Int(0..10); `rs[6]` fits. Arithmetic is no literal (§7.4): `rs[5 * 4]` is an Int key, with no static finding.
-- a/a.canon --
package a

local record R {
  n: Int(0..10)
}

local let rs: [R] keyed by n = [{ n: 6 }]

local let over: R = rs[20]

local let found: Bool = rs.find(20) != none

local let product: R = rs[5 * 4]

local let fits: R = rs[6]
-- findings.txt --
error[E3204]  a/a.canon:9:24
  20 is outside 0..10
  expected by a/a.canon:9 (over: R)

error[E3204]  a/a.canon:11:33
  20 is outside 0..10
  expected by a/a.canon:11 (found: Bool)

2 errors, 0 warnings in 1 package (…)
